00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:32.510
<v Speaker 1>Welcome back to our review of the year 2007. Well, the action got underway at Worlds on Thursday, but first there was the small matter of the Hall of Fame. The third year class was always going to be a classic and we weren't disappointed. In the induction ceremony, we were reminded of the standout qualities of Randy Bueller, Nikolai Hertzog, Sayashi Fajita has V Moscowitz, and the German juggernaut Kai Buddha.</v>

00:00:32.860 --> 00:01:03.600
<v Speaker 1>Four of these would play in the main event with Randy unable to participate due to his working for Wizards. Now. None of them had spectacular weekends or those V at 11 and five came closest to bringing back the past with an eye catch and return to the pro tour. That suggests he may have the chops to compete again at the highest level. With five incredible rings newly nestled on five talented hands, it was time to get the ball rolling with Standard. Our path to glory began with the Venezuelan</v>

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:34.630
<v Speaker 1>third seed Danielle Daversa playing fairies. He faced a blue Black Mirror of sorts in Bulgaria's Nicola Belief. There always rumours circulating at constructed events have some new big thing about to be unveiled and Worlds was no exception. In the event there really was a new big thing and it turned out to be an old new big thing. Dragon Storm. Yes, a year after Machito Mahara successfully counted to 9 and took away $50,000,</v>

00:01:34.710 --> 00:02:04.960
<v Speaker 1>the big bad Storm of Doom was back to terrorise the top tables. This time around there was no blue support for the deck, with top cards like Remand and Telling Time vanishing with Ravnica Block out of Standard. Instead, the cunning plan involved large amounts of cheap red burn spells, Incinerate Shock, Tarfire and the Suspendable Rift Bolt, plus Manor Acceleration, Lotus Bloom and Right of Flame. These were coupled with the essential combo out of the</v>

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:34.950
<v Speaker 1>Aussie Storm deck from the Summers national season, featuring Pyromancer's Swath from Future Sight and Time Spirals. Grapeshot 4 Bogardan Hellkite later, and the recipe for Dragonstorm success was almost made. But it also required something crucial, a totally unfair way to get Dragonstorm into play. Step forward Spinerock Knoll, the little anticipated hideaway land that was about to hideaway something pretty devastating. 2 suspended Rift Bolts</v>

00:02:35.060 --> 00:03:02.250
<v Speaker 1>and any burn spell in hand triggered the seven damage requiring null, and if you are lucky, you had a Dragon Storm under there for at least a count of four. Even putting a Hell Kite behind the Knoll was a severe beating for most opponents. Although none of the Dragonstorm players made it to five and go, all had positive records including Gabriel Nassif, John Finkel, Dave Williams, Mark Herberholz and Patrick Chapin, the innovator of Star City Games fame.</v>

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:33.280
<v Speaker 1>In round two, Venezuelan Diversa made it two and oh with the victory over the red green Manor ramp deck played by Hoy Chick of Hong Kong. But in round three he faced poker race Dave Williams here by sponsors invitation to play Williams. Dragonstorm went to three and oh and the path to glory moved to home turf for the first time. There was a real marquee match up for Williams in round 4 as he faced Louis Scott Vargas, but the Californians green red mana ramp deck</v>

00:03:33.370 --> 00:04:03.810
<v Speaker 1>with its enormous men were no match for the new tech on the block and Williams moved to four and O. In round five he would face San Diego champ Chris Lackman piloting a green red Snow deck. Now this deck had some quality burn spells too, Incinerate Scred and Molten Disaster as a game, ending her, well, disaster. It also had a monstrous suite of monsters, including Hell Kites of its own, the invocable Cloudthresher, Early Manner Man, Wall of Roots,</v>

