<v Speaker 1>the inevitable Time Mcgough, and even 10th Edition standout Siege Gang. Commander Lackman had ambitions to overhaul your Watanabe in the Rookie of the Year race, and he made the perfect start, acing Standard and moving to draft Law in with five wins in the bank. One of the most entertaining games of day one featured Lackman's Protocol winning partner Jacob van Lunen up against 2007 Grand Prix winner Nikolai Potter win of Russia. The Russian had a fairy deck that was pretty conventional,</v>

