<v Speaker 1>Day two opened with three more rounds of Draft 5. Players managed another three and O pod gaining them 18 points for the format. Masami Kanako from Japan had demonstrated his considerable talents in winning Grand Prix Florence. Now here was proof that his limited game was also strong. Akira Asahara had multiple Grand Prix wins behind him and was in the world's top 8 when Murray was crowned in O5. Adam Chambers of the US was part of one of the best team names</v>

00:26:51.180 --> 00:27:21.070
<v Speaker 1>ever, Gindy Sisters Fan Club when finishing runner up at Grand Prix Chicago in 2004 and was a solid Level 3 pro could issued the tribal mechanics favoured by most to seek out that old standby of quality drafters everywhere. Removal, removal and more removal. The least known of the five was Brazilian Rubens Campana and that left Frank Karsten to find Carsten acing a format what Worlds was no surprise to find that it was draft</v>

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:47.630
<v Speaker 1>What's slightly more so since the Netherlands, Starwood has a reputation for play testing and deck building second to almost no one on Earth. Having finished runner up in 2005 behind Morrie in Yokohama, you can be sure that plenty of people were looking over their shoulder at the tenacious Dutchy. However, a thoroughly subpar 2 and 3 performance in Standard meant that even with that six and O run, Carston was still in 37th place. With a lot of work to do</v>

00:27:48.690 --> 00:28:19.000
<v Speaker 1>on the path to glory, Katsuhiro Mori opened up day two against American Sam Stein. Murray had the well known cross curve pairing of cheap Kithkin and expensive giants sandwiched together with everyone's favorite filling changelings. Stein had gone blue white and had the Super powerful austere command should things get out of hand. Well, unfortunately for him, things did indeed get out of hand as Murray's deck proved too fast, too big and too well, everything to be derailed.</v>

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:50.300
<v Speaker 1>Round 10 saw a rematch of Morrie against Huber from the previous evening. This time Huber would have the chance to see if his Blue white deck would stack up better against Mary's Red white. This time the Swiss emerged triumphant and the path to glory came to Europe for the first time after 10 rounds. In the final round of draft he faced Kazuya Mitamura, still very much in the race and with the deeply sick Double Guile lurking in his deck. If Huber killed one, it probably wouldn't take Mitamura long to find one again.</v>

00:28:50.420 --> 00:29:10.550
<v Speaker 1>Thanks to the elemental incarnations back to the deck ability. Despite the enormous blue bombs, Huber was able to pull out a 21V and as much of a surprise to himself as others, he was sitting alone on top of the standings. The last remaining player undefeated at ten wins and that draw on day one with Murray</v>

00:29:11.320 --> 00:29:42.170
<v Speaker 1>a point behind was Nakano, still very much holding things together. And then on 28 came Brazilian Rubens Campana, galloping up the standings thanks to his six and O draft run. Mitamura, Canaco and Chapin were amongst those on nine and two with 2003 Japanese National champion Qatar Otsuka also in the mix. The big name to join the fray was Frenchman Gabriel Nassif, looking to make back-to-back top eights at Worlds whilst this Reli Peleg was still fronting a strong</v>

00:29:42.240 --> 00:30:12.200
<v Speaker 1>challenge. The Israeli squad had advanced to 2nd place now, but the Swiss quartet was seemingly galloping away with things a clear 10 points ahead of their nearest rivals. Japan were third, while Austria were up to 4th with the Netherlands. Then it was Belgian, the Russian Federation were in 7th and GB Tallyho we're up to 8th. France were well down the field and Germany weren't doing much either. In the Player of the Year race, Gillenwater Tapper and Raphael Levy had pulled around clear of Saito on</v>

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:28.470
<v Speaker 1>eight wins and three losses, but they would still need Saito to miss top 8 before they could contemplate a potential smash and grab raid on the trophy. For Cheon and Samura, the rounds were running out as we headed to the final format for the individual event, Legacy.</v>

00:30:31.950 --> 00:31:01.840
<v Speaker 1>It was in round twelve, the first round of Legacy, that the Player of the Year door would finally be left ajar by Tomoharu Saito, while he could only manage a draw against Spaniard Fernandez. Rafael Levy defeated Yoga Vigesimal in a repeat of their Invitational clash and Guillaume Wafo Tapa beat Belgium Stan van der Velden by two to one. Now both his rivals knew that Cyto now couldn't mathematically make the final table. Levy in particular was on fire,</v>

00:31:01.950 --> 00:31:31.990
<v Speaker 1>having started out three and oh, he then lost the last two standard rounds and the first law in draft round to be at a less than stellar 3 and three. From there, though, he had literally been invincible. Nobody could touch him as he won round after round after round. He defeated Chris Lackman in round 13 to extend his winning streak to seven straight, a phenomenal achievement at this level. 8 players would ultimately achieve a perfect sweep 5 and O in Legacy. Three of these 8</v>

00:31:32.060 --> 00:32:03.520
<v Speaker 1>the threshold decks designed to get mileage out of the eponymous mechanic. These included Shelter Yasuoka of Japan, Belgium's Man on the Rise, Moraine Liberte, and Croatian Gregor Petrich Maritech. 2 undefeated players went with Cephalad decks. These found very naughty ways to abuse Cephalid Illusionist, dumping entire libraries into the graveyard at top speed before returning Sutured Ghoul to play. Since that wouldn't in and of itself do, the needful Dragon Wings ensured that the goal was fully hasted up</v>

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:21.060
<v Speaker 1>and proceeded on its way to demolish the opponent. Daniel O'mahoney Schwartz, also known as OMS from Days Gone My piloted a deck featuring the same card selection engine that had served Remy 48 so well in winning Protor Valencia, namely Counterbalance and Sensei's Divining Top</v>

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:51.450
<v Speaker 1>with Dark Confidant on hand to generate card advantage by the bucket load. This was a mean deck that also sported the ultimate white creature removal spell, the much loved Swords to Plowshares. That left two more 5 and O decks from China. Jahu was improbably playing Suicide Black, featuring such old school delights as Carnage Phage and Sarcoma Nancy for top speed beats backed up with some serious hand disruption. Thoughtseize, Duress him to Turak</v>

00:32:51.660 --> 00:33:13.850
<v Speaker 1>and remember this is a format where Dark Ritual still legal. That left Stuart Wright of Great Britain last year. He went six and oh in extended and but for A33 performance in draft he would have made top 8 here. His Welder Survival deck ran rings round most of his opponents and rights of Blair, who can manipulate graveyards to good effect like few others.</v>

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:27.190
<v Speaker 1>Now at 15 and one in his last 16 constructed matches at Worlds, few can doubt his playing credentials. Despite missing out on that top 8 on tie breaks, at least he's a fully fledged Level 3 next year.</v>

00:33:28.450 --> 00:33:58.280
<v Speaker 1>Thankfully, there was a wide variety of decks represented in Legacy, and it looked like a seriously tasty and fun format. In addition to the five and O decks already mentioned, four and one results were posted by two Land Belcher using Goblin Charbelcher to burn out an opponent. Straight up Goblins charging around. There were assorted Prison style decks and Assault Loan, combining Ravnica's life from the Loam with the nasty Seismic Assault for an endless supply of damage. Then there were</v>

00:33:58.350 --> 00:34:12.840
<v Speaker 1>Enchantress decks, a Manner Bond deck piloted by Rude Warmenhoven Yoga Vegas MA running Allure and it was a really diverse field. With the success of Legacy here at Worlds, it may not be long before we see a full on Legacy Protocol</v>

00:34:13.910 --> 00:34:43.660
<v Speaker 1>4. Legacy Tournament leader Christoph Huber had chosen a mono white prison deck and against Brazil's Rubens Campana it was enough to overcome his threshold. Round 13 saw him face Patrick Chapin, but Chapin defeated the Swiss player by two to one. The path to glory was back in America but only for one round has Chapin found himself up against Gabriel Nassif who would not be denied. Nasif had a variation of a land still controlled Eck, and with his victory over Chapin</v>

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:55.210
<v Speaker 1>he was assured a place in the top eight once he intentionally drew with Nagano in round 15. This guaranteed that the path to glory would commence Sunday in France with Nasief.</v>

00:34:56.230 --> 00:35:26.800
<v Speaker 1>As the rounds went on, Levy and Wafa Tapa continued to keep the dream alive, whilst around 14 loss to Roll Van Hazak put Levy almost certainly out of contention barring a tiebreak miracle, Gillian Wafa Tapa marched on defeating Manuel Buca, yet another member of the Swiss team having a belting run. Wafa Tapa knew that around 15 victory against Levies conqueror Van Hazak would see him into the top 8. To say that Wafer Taba had gone for a metagame approach to Legacy would be putting it mildly.</v>

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:57.620
<v Speaker 1>He was playing both Blood Moon and Magus of the Moon main deck in a monored concoction that featured plenty of cards that you wouldn't expect to necessarily see in a block constructed deck. Simian spirit Guide? Gathan Raiders Arc Slogger Rakdos Pit Dragon? He you kidding me? Still, if Wafa Tapa makes the call, you kind of think he probably knows more than you do. Against Van Hazak he found himself one nil down and facing an abysmal Mulligan.</v>

00:35:57.700 --> 00:36:28.250
<v Speaker 1>Just 4 cards. Now that is an almost unwinnable game right there. But when you add in the fact that the Dutchie was playing heavy discard, it became surely impossible. Apparently not, as Wafa Tapa climbed out of the mammoth hole he was in to force game three. A lot of people at that point would say it wasn't their destiny, but having disposed of Levi's top 8 ambitions, the likeable Van Hazmat was in no mood to have his own dreams crushed. He dispatched Wafa Tapper in an 1 sided game</v>

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:58.120
<v Speaker 1>three and the Frenchman would also succumb to Stuart Wright in the final round. At that point, the final threat to Tomoharu Saito was extinguished and the ecstatic Japanese Level 6 could revel in the title Player of the Year You Your Watanabe also saw off his opponents to claim the Rookie of the Year title in the team event was still a full day of nation V Nation action to come. Things were getting tight now. Switzerland had been joined at the top by Japan, with Israel a clear third,</v>

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:14.450
<v Speaker 1>Stewart 4th and Chile were continuing to show strongly in fifth. Then came Russia, the Netherlands, Great Britain, Norway and the Czech Republic, but all of that could change on the team day. As for the individual top eight, well, you'll just have to wait for Part 3, won't you?</v>

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:47.350
<v Speaker 1>In many ways, the team day of Worlds is the most fun. Even those lucky few who have made it to the Sunday showdown can relax somewhat in the company of their teammates. And although you'll never find a room full of magic plays where there isn't some degree of rivalry, the emphasis on team day is very much about sporting behaviour, friendly rivalry and a shared enjoyment of the great event at the sharp end. Where national teams were striving hard to make the final on Sunday, it was a frustrating day by and large, with 9 points for a team win</v>

00:37:47.480 --> 00:38:19.340
<v Speaker 1>only being secured each round if both 2 headed giant pairings won their jewel. Making progress up the leaderboard was extremely difficult. As an example, with only one defeat in six rounds, the British team managed to secure fewer points than if they'd won two matches and lost 4. The Swiss, who had gone into the day as joint leaders, quickly established a lead again, generating the full 18 points available from the first two rounds of the day. Since this left them a massive 19 points clear of the third place</v>

00:38:19.410 --> 00:38:49.850
<v Speaker 1>Tech Republic, it seemed almost certain that Switzerland would be into the final. Behind them are mighty scrap was developing between last year's finalists Japan and the Netherlands, plus the Israelis and the Czechs. With two rounds to go and the final pairs of teammates about to embark on the last draft of the day, the Swiss were indeed home and host, having frankly obliterated anything that moved. Israel and the Czechs finally fell away, and that left Austria, Japan and the Netherlands to compete</v>

00:38:49.920 --> 00:39:20.040
<v Speaker 1>for the second final slot in the penultimate round. The Japanese played spoiler, denying victory to their Dutch opponents. But if the Dutch could defeat the Swiss in a potential final preview in the last round, then it would all hinge on whether the Japanese could also defeat Austria. Well, in the event, neither contender could get a point in the final round. And so Austria advanced, but by the narrowest of margins from Canada, whose squad of Andrew Tingay, Daniel Kramer, Mike Arenson</v>

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:42.490
<v Speaker 1>in a certain rich hound, had chomped their way up the table to finish an outstanding 3rd. And so the table was set for the final Super Sunday of 2007 with both individual and team World titles on the line. And you can find out how it all went down when we return from Part 3 of the Magic Review of the Year 2007.</v>

